---
<Docs
  title="Levni Node API: compose, render, animate, boards, posters and brand kits"
  description="Use Levni from JavaScript: compose a scene to SVG, render it to PNG or WebP, animate it as GIF, WebP or MP4, read the layout critic, search the library, and make boards, posters, storyboards and brand kits."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Use Levni</p>
  <h1>Node API</h1>
  <p class="lede">Everything the CLI does is a function. Use it in a build step, a CMS hook, a script or your own MCP server. Node.js 20 or newer, ES modules.</p>

  <h2 id="import">Import</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npm install @levni/levni</code></pre>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { compose, critique, render } from '@levni/levni';`}</code></pre>
  <p>The package is ESM only. From a CommonJS file, use <code>await import('@levni/levni')</code>. Animation, storyboards and brand kits need 0.5.0 or newer (see <a href="/docs/getting-started/#install">install</a>).</p>

  <h2 id="render">Render a scene</h2>
  <p><code>render(scene, out, opts?)</code> draws a scene and writes the file. The extension picks the format. It returns the critic’s notes and the SVG.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { render } from '@levni/levni';

const scene = {
  seed: 4,
  elements: [{ type: 'prop', name: 'rocket', x: 600, y: 336, size: 360, shadow: true }],
};

const { notes } = await render(scene, 'rocket.png');           // or .webp, .svg
if (notes.length) console.warn(notes.join('\\n'));              // the critic
await render(scene, 'rocket.png', { preview: true });          // also rocket.prev.jpg (560 px)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Options: <code>preview</code> (PNG only) writes a small JPEG next to the file; <code>webp</code> is passed to the WebP encoder (<code>budgetKB</code>, <code>startQ</code>, <code>minQ</code>, <code>step</code>).</p>

  <h2 id="compose">Compose and critique</h2>
  <p><code>compose(scene)</code> returns the picture as an SVG string without writing anything, plus <code>used</code>, the boxes of everything it drew, and the <code>canvas</code>. <code>critique(used, canvas)</code> turns those boxes into layout notes.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { compose, critique } from '@levni/levni';
import sharp from 'sharp';

const { svg, used, canvas } = await compose(scene);
const notes = critique(used, canvas);          // [] when the layout is fine
const png = await sharp(Buffer.from(svg)).png().toBuffer();`}</code></pre>
  <p>Scenes are plain objects, so build them in code. The same <code>seed</code> always gives the same picture, which makes renders safe to cache by scene hash.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`const sizes = { og: 'og', square: 'square', story: 'story' };
for (const [name, format] of Object.entries(sizes)) {
  await render({ ...scene, format, reflow: true }, \`cover-\${name}.webp\`);
}`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>format</code> with <code>reflow: true</code> recomposes the scene for another canvas instead of cropping it. See <a href="/docs/scenes/#formats-and-reflow">Formats and reflow</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="props">Work with drawings</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { loadProps, findProp, validateProp, measureVariant, addPropDir } from '@levni/levni';

const all = loadProps();                       // { rocket: { name, category, tags, variants }, ... }
const rocket = findProp('roket');              // names and tags match, in any language
const chair = findProp('armchair-2');          // an old name finds the renamed drawing (armchair-padded)
console.log(rocket.name, rocket.variants.length);

addPropDir('./my-drawings');                   // include your own folder
const problems = validateProp(myProp);         // [] when it is valid
const { size, warnings } = measureVariant(myProp.variants[0]);`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="brand">Boards and posters</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { renderBoard, expandBrand, renderPosters, expandPosters } from '@levni/levni';

// one call, nine tiles
const brand = { name: 'Feneri', tagline: 'Slow ferries across the Bosphorus', mark: 'lighthouse',
                inks: ['#14213D', '#FCA311', '#3A86A8', '#EFEAE0'] };
const { width, height, bytes } = await renderBoard({ brand }, 'board.png');

const spec = expandBrand(brand);               // the full board spec, to print and edit

// eight posters and series.png
const { files } = await renderPosters({ brand: { ...brand, words: ['Sail', 'Drift', 'Land', 'Return'] } }, 'posters/');
const list = expandPosters({ brand });         // [{ template, scene }, ...]: edit a scene, then render(scene, ...)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Formats: <a href="/docs/boards/">Brand boards</a> and <a href="/docs/posters/">Branding posters</a>. <code>renderBoard</code> takes <code>{`{ baseDir }`}</code> to resolve scene paths in a spec.</p>

  <h2 id="animation">Animate a scene</h2>
  <p>Frame <code>i</code> of <code>N</code> is drawn at <code>t = i / N</code>, so the loop closes. <code>animate</code> writes a GIF or WebP; <code>renderFrames</code> gives you the frames as PNG buffers for your own encoder or canvas. The options and the motion fields are on <a href="/docs/animation/">Animation</a>.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { animate, renderFrames, framePlan, frameStrip } from '@levni/levni';

const { notes, bytes } = await animate(scene, 'loop.gif', { frames: 24, fps: 12, width: 800 });

framePlan(scene, { frames: 4 });               // [{ t: 0 }, { t: 0.25 }, { t: 0.5 }, { t: 0.75 }]
const { frames, width, height, delay, moving } = await renderFrames(scene, { frames: 24, width: 800 });
const strip = await frameStrip(frames, { count: 6, width: 280 });   // one PNG to look at`}</code></pre>
  <p>The scene can hold <code>motion</code> on any element, <code>stagger</code> and <code>camera</code>. <code>MOTIONS</code> lists the motion names and <code>EASE</code> holds the easing functions by name. <code>encodeGif</code> is the GIF encoder <code>animate</code> uses.</p>
  <p>A moving README banner starts from <code>bannerScene</code>, which lives in the <code>banner</code> subpath:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { animate } from '@levni/levni';
import { bannerScene } from '@levni/levni/banner';

const banner = bannerScene({ owner: 'you', name: 'your-repo', description: 'One line.', mark: 'rocket', motion: true });
await animate(banner, 'banner.gif', { frames: 36, width: 800 });`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="sequences">Frames, MP4, sprites and tiles</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { writeFrames, spriteSheet, toMp4, ffmpegAvailable, render, transparent, tileable, tileCheck } from '@levni/levni';

await writeFrames(scene, 'public/hero', { frames: 24, fps: 12 });                    // frame-0001.png… and frames.json
await spriteSheet(scene, 'hero-sheet.png', { frames: 8, fps: 12, width: 256, columns: 4 });
if (ffmpegAvailable()) await toMp4(scene, 'hero.mp4', { frames: 48, fps: 24, loops: 3, crf: 20 });

await render(transparent(scene), 'sticker.png');                                    // clear ground
const png = await tileable(scene, { size: 512 });                                    // seamless square
console.log(await tileCheck(png));                                                   // { seamX, seamY }`}</code></pre>
  <p>Details, the atlas layout and recipes for Remotion, three.js and Blender are on <a href="/docs/export/">Export extras</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="storyboards-kits">Storyboards and brand kits</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { expandStoryboard, renderStoryboard, renderKit, expandKit, brandFromUrl } from '@levni/levni';

const shots = expandStoryboard(spec);                                                // plain JSON: edit a scene, render it
const { width, height, total } = await renderStoryboard(spec, 'story.png', { columns: 3, framesDir: 'frames' });

const found = await brandFromUrl('https://example.com');                             // fetches that one page
const { files, notes } = await renderKit({ brand: found }, 'kit/');                  // or { brand: { name, inks, … } }
const plan = expandKit({ brand: { name: 'Kumru' } });                                // { brand, assets }, nothing rendered`}</code></pre>
  <p>Formats: <a href="/docs/storyboards/">Storyboards</a> and <a href="/docs/brand-kits/">Brand kits</a>. <code>brandFromUrl</code> fetches exactly the address you give, with a 10 second timeout, and nothing else.</p>

  <h2 id="export">Export and print passes</h2>
  <p>If you hold an SVG already, <code>toPng</code> and <code>toWebp</code> rasterise it, both through <code>rasterize</code>: the picture is drawn in three plain layers and the crayon finish is applied to the pixels in JavaScript, which is about seven times faster than the SVG renderer's own filter with the same result. Use <code>{'rasterize(svg, { density, raw })'}</code> directly for buffers, thumbnails or your own pipeline. A scene’s <code>screen</code> field runs an ordered-dither print pass after the raster; <code>screenRaster</code> exposes it.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { compose, toWebp, toPng } from '@levni/levni';

const { svg } = await compose({ ...scene, screen: 'gameboy' });
await toWebp(svg, 'out.webp', { budgetKB: 80, screen: 'gameboy' });
await toPng(svg, 'out.png', { screen: 'gameboy' });`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="exports">All exports</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Exports, scrolls sideways"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Export</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{exportsRows.map(([a, b]) => <tr><td><code>{a}</code></td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="subpaths">Subpath imports</h2>
  <p>The package also exposes narrower entry points: <code>@levni/levni/props</code>, <code>/critic</code>, <code>/board</code>, <code>/poster</code>, <code>/judge</code> and <code>/mcp</code>. New in 0.5.0: <code>/animate</code>, <code>/sequence</code>, <code>/storyboard</code>, <code>/brandkit</code>, <code>/banner</code> and <code>/gif</code>, plus <code>/browser</code> and <code>/vite</code> for running the engine in a web page.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Subpaths, scrolls sideways"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Subpath</th><th>What is in it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>/animate</code></td><td><code>animate</code>, <code>renderFrames</code>, <code>framePlan</code>, <code>frameStrip</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/sequence</code></td><td><code>writeFrames</code>, <code>spriteSheet</code>, <code>toMp4</code>, <code>ffmpegAvailable</code>, <code>transparent</code>, <code>tileable</code>, <code>tileCheck</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/storyboard</code></td><td><code>expandStoryboard</code>, <code>renderStoryboard</code>, <code>timecode</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/brandkit</code></td><td><code>renderKit</code>, <code>expandKit</code>, <code>brandFromHtml</code>, <code>brandFromUrl</code>, <code>resolveBrand</code>, <code>seededInks</code>, <code>KIT_ASSETS</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/banner</code></td><td><code>bannerScene</code>, <code>pickMark</code>, <code>repoTerms</code>, <code>hashSeed</code>, <code>BANNER_SIZE</code>, <code>BANNER_THEMES</code>, <code>FALLBACK_MARKS</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/gif</code></td><td><code>encodeGif</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/browser</code>, <code>/vite</code></td><td>The engine for a web page, and the Vite plugin that bundles it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <pre><code class="language-js">{`import { critique } from '@levni/levni/critic';
import { bannerScene } from '@levni/levni/banner';
import { serve } from '@levni/levni/mcp';     // run the MCP server from your own process`}</code></pre>
</Docs>
